Q: JUnit Test cases failing after upgrading to Gradle 9, Springboot 4 and Java 25 A: <p>The error</p>
<blockquote>
<p>java.lang.NoClassDefFoundError: org/springframework/http/HttpStatusCode</p>
</blockquote>
<p>occurs when your runtime application attempts to load the <code>HttpStatusCode</code> interface, but cannot find it in the classpath. This is almost always caused by a Spring Framework version mismatch between Spring 5 (or earlier) and Spring 6 (Spring Boot 3).</p>
<p>The <code>HttpStatusCode</code> interface was introduced in Spring 6.0 / Spring Boot 3.0 to decouple status codes from the standard <code>HttpStatus</code> enum.</p>
<p>You have a dependency, compiled against Spring 6 / Spring Boot 3, but your project is still running on Spring 5 / Spring Boot 2 (where <code>HttpStatusCode</code> does not exist).</p>
<p>To fix this you have two approaches:</p>
<ol>
<li><p>You want to stay on Spring Boot 2 / Spring 5. If you cannot upgrade your main project right now, you must downgrade the specific dependency that is triggering the error, so that it uses Spring 5-compatible classes. Check your dependency tree (<code>mvn dependency:tree</code> or <code>./gradlew dependencies</code>) to see what library is forcing the Spring 6 compiled code. Downgrade libraries like <code>springdoc-openapi</code>, <code>spring-web</code>, or generated clients to versions released prior to late 2022.</p>
</li>
<li><p>You want to upgrade to Spring Boot 3 / Spring 6. If you are actively trying to migrate to Spring Boot 3, your classpath still contains lingering transitive dependencies from Spring Boot 2. Force clean your build cache: Run <code>mvn clean</code> or <code>./gradlew clean</code> to ensure old artifacts are deleted. Align your BOM: Ensure your parent POM or dependency management explicitly manages your versions to avoid mixed dependency states. Exclude old dependencies: if a third-party library is bringing in <code>spring-web version 5.x</code> transitively, exclude it:</p>
</li>
</ol>
<pre class="lang-xml prettyprint-override"><code>&lt;!-- Maven Example --&gt;
&lt;dependency&gt;
    &lt;groupId&gt;some.problematic.library&lt;/groupId&gt;
    &lt;artifactId&gt;library-name&lt;/artifactId&gt;
    &lt;version&gt;1.0.0&lt;/version&gt;
    &lt;exclusions&gt;
        &lt;exclusion&gt;
            &lt;groupId&gt;org.springframework&lt;/groupId&gt;
            &lt;artifactId&gt;spring-web&lt;/artifactId&gt;
        &lt;/exclusion&gt;
    &lt;/exclusions&gt;
&lt;/dependency
</code></pre>
